=== FILE: components/protocols/start-review.md ===
---
name: start-review
trigger: "start code review"
aliases:
  - "review this PR"
  - "review this MR"
  - "submit review"
  - "do code review"
---

## What this protocol does

Reads the current MR/PR diff, applies the review checklist, posts the structured review comment to GitLab or GitHub, and updates the Last Review artifact.

## Steps

0. **Load config first.** Read `{{install_dir}}/config.json`. Extract:
   `platform`, `project_ref`, `api_token`, `base_url`. The
   `{{install_dir}}` placeholder is substituted to an absolute path at
   install time by the runtime adapter. If the literal `{{install_dir}}`
   remains here, the substitution step was skipped — stop and tell the user.
1. Ask: "What is the MR/PR number (or URL)?"
2. Fetch the diff:
   - **GitLab**: `GET {{config.base_url}}/api/v4/projects/{{config.project_ref}}/merge_requests/<iid>/changes` with header `PRIVATE-TOKEN: {{config.api_token}}`
   - **GitHub**: `GET https://api.github.com/repos/{{config.project_ref}}/pulls/<number>/files` with header `Authorization: Bearer {{config.api_token}}`
3. Read the diff and apply every checklist category from the `code-review` skill (Correctness, Security, Performance, Code quality, Tests, Breaking changes, Documentation).
4. Draft the structured review comment using the output format defined in the `code-review` skill. Assign a verdict: **Approved**, **Needs Changes**, or **Commented**.
5. Show the full draft to the user and ask: "Shall I post this review?"
6. On confirmation, run script `post_review` with arguments: `{{install_dir}}/config.json` `<mr_number>` `<review_body>` `<verdict>`. (The script re-reads config.json itself; pass the same path you used in Step 0.)
7. Update the Last Review artifact with the review data. Build this JSON object first:
   ```json
   { "mr": <number>, "verdict": "<verdict>", "notes": "<one-line summary>",
     "link": "<URL from post_review output>", "ts": "<ISO 8601 timestamp>" }
   ```
   Then update using whichever method fits the current platform:

   - **Cowork**: call `mcp__cowork__list_artifacts` to find the artifact named "last-review",
     then call `mcp__cowork__update_artifact` with the artifact ID. In the updated HTML,
     replace the content between `/* DATA_START */` and `/* DATA_END */` with:
     `var LAST_REVIEW = <JSON object>;`

   - **Claude Code / Codex / Cursor / any platform with file write access**:
     locate the installed `artifact.html` (same directory as `SKILL.md`), read it,
     replace the content between `/* DATA_START */` and `/* DATA_END */` with:
     `var LAST_REVIEW = <JSON object>;`
     Write the file back. The user can open it in any browser to see the card.
     Also update the table in `artifact.md` with the same values.

   - **No file access / CLI-only**: show the updated `artifact.md` table in chat
     so the user has a record of the review.

8. Confirm to the user: "Review posted -- see it at [link]."

## Config values used

- `{{config.platform}}` -- `gitlab` or `github`
- `{{config.project_ref}}` -- GitLab numeric project ID or GitHub `owner/repo` slug
- `{{config.api_token}}` -- Personal access token (read from env var)
- `{{config.base_url}}` -- GitLab base URL (only when platform is `gitlab`)

## Outputs

- A review comment posted to the MR/PR on GitLab or GitHub
- Last Review artifact updated: HTML card (all file-capable platforms) and/or artifact.md table

=== END FILE ===

=== FILE: components/skills/code-review/SKILL.md ===
---
name: code-review
description: "Reads an MR/PR diff, applies a 7-category review checklist, posts the structured review to GitLab or GitHub, and updates the Last Review record. Triggers: 'start code review', 'review this PR', 'review this MR', 'submit review', 'do code review'."
---

# code-review

## When to use this skill

Apply this skill whenever the user says one of the trigger phrases above —
"start code review", "review this PR", "review this MR", "submit review",
"do code review" — or otherwise asks for a code review of a merge / pull
request.

This skill is self-contained at runtime: it covers the full flow (read
config → fetch diff → review → confirm → post → update Last Review record),
plus the checklist, verdict criteria, and output format used for the review
itself.

---

## Runtime setup — load config

**Run this once at session start, before responding to any trigger phrase.**

1. Read `{{install_dir}}/config.json`. The absolute install_dir is baked into
   this SKILL.md at install time by the runtime adapter — by the time you
   read this file, `{{install_dir}}` is a real absolute path (e.g.
   `C:/Users/USERNAME/.claude/packages/ai-code-review-flow/` on Cowork or
   `C:/Users/USERNAME/.codex/packages/ai-code-review-flow/` on Codex).

   If the literal string `{{install_dir}}` appears below instead of an
   absolute path, the install adapter's substitution step was skipped — stop
   and tell the user the install is broken; do not try to guess the path.

2. From config.json, load these keys into working memory for the session:
   - `platform` — `gitlab` or `github`
   - `project_ref` — GitLab numeric project ID, or GitHub `owner/repo` slug
   - `api_token` — personal access token (treat as sensitive: do not echo,
     do not include in logs or chat output)
   - `base_url` — present only when `platform == gitlab`; defaults to
     `https://gitlab.com` if absent or empty

3. If config.json is missing, the install never completed past INSTALL.md
   Step 6 (Write config.json). Tell the user:
   > "I don't see config for this package at `{{install_dir}}/config.json`.
   > The package may not be fully installed for this user yet — should I run
   > the install wizard now?"
   Point them at the bundle's INSTALL.md / CONFIGURE.md to complete a fresh
   install. Do not improvise config values.

The path to config.json (`{{install_dir}}/config.json`) is also what you pass
as the first argument to `scripts/post_review.py` later — keep it handy.

---

## Workflow — start-review

Triggered by any of: "start code review", "review this PR", "review this MR",
"submit review", "do code review".

### Step 1 — Ask for the MR/PR

> "What is the MR/PR number (or URL)?"

If the user gives a URL, parse the number from it. If they give just a
number, use it directly.

### Step 2 — Fetch the diff

**GitLab** (`platform == gitlab`):

```
GET {base_url}/api/v4/projects/{project_ref}/merge_requests/<iid>/changes
Header: PRIVATE-TOKEN: {api_token}
```

**GitHub** (`platform == github`):

```
GET https://api.github.com/repos/{project_ref}/pulls/<number>/files
Header: Authorization: Bearer {api_token}
```

Use the values loaded in Runtime setup. Never paste the api_token into chat
output.

### Step 3 — Read the diff and apply the checklist

Work through each category in order. Note issues as you find them; group
them in the final comment.

#### 1. Correctness
- Does the code do what the MR/PR description says it should?
- Are there off-by-one errors, null/None dereferences, or unhandled edge cases?
- Do any changed functions have callers not updated to match the new signature?
- Are error return values and exceptions handled appropriately?

#### 2. Security
- Is any user-controlled input validated or sanitised before use?
- Are secrets, tokens, or credentials ever hardcoded or logged?
- Are SQL queries, shell commands, or template strings constructed safely
  (no injection risk)?
- Are file paths validated to prevent directory traversal?

#### 3. Performance
- Are there N+1 query patterns or loops that make repeated API/DB calls?
- Are large data structures built in memory when streaming would suffice?
- Are expensive operations inside tight loops where they could be hoisted?

#### 4. Code quality
- Are functions and variables named clearly and consistently with the
  existing codebase?
- Is duplicated logic introduced that could be extracted?
- Is any commented-out code or debug print left in?
- Is the change self-contained — does it avoid unrelated refactors mixed
  into the diff?

#### 5. Tests
- Does the diff include tests for the new or changed behaviour?
- Do the tests cover the happy path and at least one error/edge case?
- Are existing tests broken by the change (look for deleted assertions or
  changed expectations)?

#### 6. Breaking changes
- Does the change alter a public API, config key, or env-var contract?
- Are database migrations included if the schema changed?
- Is backwards compatibility preserved, or is a migration/deprecation path
  documented?

#### 7. Documentation
- Are new public functions/classes documented (docstring, JSDoc, etc.)?
- Is the README or inline docs updated if the change affects user-visible
  behaviour?
- Does the MR/PR description clearly explain *why* the change is needed,
  not just *what* it does?

### Step 4 — Assign a verdict and draft the comment

**Verdict criteria:**

| Verdict | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **Approved** | No blocking issues; minor comments are advisory only. |
| **Needs Changes** | One or more blocking issues must be resolved before merge. |
| **Commented** | Observations shared but no explicit approval or block requested. |

Use **Needs Changes** for: security issues, correctness bugs, missing tests
for critical paths, breaking changes without a migration path.
Use **Approved** when you would be comfortable merging as-is.
Use **Commented** for informational feedback or when the review is
intentionally partial.

**Output format** — structure the comment exactly as follows:

```
## Code Review -- [MR/PR title or number]

**Verdict:** [Approved | Needs Changes | Commented]

### Summary
[2-4 sentence plain-language summary of the change and your overall impression.]

### Blocking issues
[List each blocking issue with file + line reference where possible. Omit section if none.]

### Suggestions
[Non-blocking improvements -- code quality, naming, test coverage gaps. Omit section if none.]

### Positive notes
[Mention what was done well -- helps the author understand what to keep doing.]
```

Keep the comment concise. Aim for actionable specifics over generic advice.
If there are no items for a section, omit that section entirely.

### Step 5 — Show the draft, ask for confirmation

Display the full draft review to the user and ask:

> "Shall I post this review?"

Wait for explicit user confirmation. Do **not** post without it.

### Step 6 — Post the review

On confirmation, invoke `scripts/post_review.py`. The script lives next to
this SKILL.md (in the runtime skill directory). Its usage is:

```
python3 scripts/post_review.py <config_json_path> <mr_number> <review_body> <verdict>
```

Use `{{install_dir}}/config.json` as `<config_json_path>` (this is the same
path you loaded in Runtime setup; the script re-reads it to pick up
`platform`, `project_ref`, `api_token`, `base_url`).

Pass the review body as a single string (multi-line OK; the script handles
it). The verdict is one of: `Approved`, `Needs Changes`, `Commented`.

Capture the URL the script prints on success — you need it for Step 7.

### Step 7 — Update the Last Review record

Build this JSON object first:

```json
{
  "mr": <number>,
  "verdict": "<verdict>",
  "notes": "<one-line summary>",
  "link": "<URL from post_review output>",
  "ts": "<ISO 8601 timestamp>"
}
```

Then update using whichever method fits the current platform:

**Cowork**: call `mcp__cowork__list_artifacts` to find the artifact named
`last-review`, then call `mcp__cowork__update_artifact` with its ID. In the
updated HTML, replace the content between `/* DATA_START */` and
`/* DATA_END */` markers with:

```javascript
var LAST_REVIEW = <JSON object>;
```

**Codex / Claude Code / Cursor / any platform with file write access**:
the Last Review record lives at `{{install_dir}}/last-review.md` (Codex,
seeded at install time by the Codex adapter Step C5) or as an installed
`artifact.html` / `artifact.md` next to this SKILL.md (other platforms).
Update both forms when present: rewrite the table in the markdown file with
the new values, and if `artifact.html` is present, also replace the content
between the `/* DATA_START */` and `/* DATA_END */` markers with the
`var LAST_REVIEW = ...;` assignment.

**No file access / CLI-only**: show the updated last-review table directly
in chat so the user has a record of the review.

### Step 8 — Confirm to the user

> "Review posted — see it at <link>."

If the Last Review update succeeded, mention that the record was refreshed.

---

## Files in this skill

After install, the runtime AI sees these files alongside `SKILL.md`:

- `scripts/post_review.py` — POSTs the review to GitLab/GitHub via REST API.
  Invoked in Workflow Step 6.
- `artifact.html` — Last Review card template. The Cowork install adapter
  creates the live artifact in Cowork's registry from this; on Codex it
  seeds `{{install_dir}}/last-review.md`. This file remains as the fallback
  source for re-creating the artifact if it gets lost.
- `artifact.md` — markdown fallback table for the Last Review record.

## Notes

- This skill is part of the `ai-code-review-flow` A3IP package.
- The api_token is sensitive — never echo it, never include it in error
  messages, never put it in the review body or the Last Review record.
- If the user asks you to post without showing the draft first, decline and
  show the draft anyway. Step 5 confirmation is non-negotiable.
- If the api_token is invalid or the project_ref is wrong, `post_review.py`
  will exit with a non-zero status and print an error to stderr. Surface that
  to the user; suggest re-running the configure wizard if the token expired.

=== END FILE ===

=== FILE: CONFIGURE.md ===
---
format: a3ip-configure
spec: "1.6"
package: ai-code-review-flow
---

# Configuration Wizard: ai-code-review-flow

You are setting up this workflow. Ask the questions below in order.
Be conversational — no need to present them as a rigid form.
Collect all required values before moving to installation.

## Before Installation — ask these first

---

### 1. Installation directory
Ask:
> "What is your Installation directory? Directory where ai-code-review-flow's config.json, installed.json, and scripts will be stored. Default is the platform convention (Cowork / Claude Code: ~/.claude/packages/<name>/, Codex: ~/.codex/packages/<name>/). IMPORTANT for the install AI: expand the user-typed value to an absolute path before writing to config.json or installed.json. On Cowork (Windows), expand `~` to the Windows user home using the platform's native separator -- e.g. ~/.claude/packages/X/ becomes the absolute path under C:/Users/USERNAME/.claude/packages/X/. On Linux/macOS, expand to /home/USERNAME/ or /Users/USERNAME/. The runtime AI reads this value via the Read tool which does NOT expand ~ -- storing a tilde here breaks the runtime config lookup. For example: ~/.claude/packages/ai-code-review-flow/"

- Key: `install_dir`
- Type: string
- Required: yes
- Default: `~/.claude/packages/ai-code-review-flow/`
- Example: `~/.claude/packages/ai-code-review-flow/`

---

### 2. Code hosting platform
Ask:
> "Which code hosting platform should be used? The platform where your code is hosted."

- Key: `platform`
- Type: select
- Required: yes
- Options:
  - `gitlab` — GitLab (gitlab.com or self-hosted) (default)
  - `github` — GitHub (github.com)

---

### 3. Project reference
Ask:
> "What is your Project reference? GitLab: numeric project ID (Settings -> General). GitHub: owner/repo slug. For example: 12345678  (GitLab)  or  acme/my-service  (GitHub)"

- Key: `project_ref`
- Type: string
- Required: yes
- Example: `12345678  (GitLab)  or  acme/my-service  (GitHub)`

---

### 4. API token
Ask:
> "What is your API token? Personal access token. GitLab: api scope. GitHub: repo scope. I won't display this value back to you."

- Key: `api_token`
- Type: string
- Required: yes
- **Sensitive** — do NOT echo the value back to the user.
  Once received, confirm only: "✅ API token received and will be stored securely in an environment variable."
  Do NOT include `{{config.api_token}}` in the confirmation summary.

---

### 5. GitLab base URL
*(Only ask if: platform == gitlab)*

Ask:
> "What is your GitLab base URL? Base URL for self-hosted GitLab. Leave blank to use gitlab.com. (optional; default: https://gitlab.com) For example: https://gitlab.mycompany.com"

- Key: `base_url`
- Type: string
- Required: no (optional)
- Default: `https://gitlab.com`
- Example: `https://gitlab.mycompany.com`
- If blank or skipped: store as empty/default, skip related steps during protocol execution.

## Confirmation

Before proceeding to installation, summarize everything and ask the user to confirm:

---
Here's what I'll configure:

- **Installation directory**: {{config.install_dir}}
- **Code hosting platform**: {{config.platform}}
- **Project reference**: {{config.project_ref}}
- **API token**: ✅ received (not displayed)
- **GitLab base URL**: {{config.base_url}}

Shall I proceed with installation?
---

Wait for explicit confirmation before moving to INSTALL.md.

=== END FILE ===

=== FILE: INSTALL.md ===
---
format: a3ip-install
spec: "1.9"
package: ai-code-review-flow
---

# Installation Guide: ai-code-review-flow

## 1. Check for Existing Installation
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the AI MUST know whether a prior installation of this
package exists on the host, and if so where its `config_dir` lives.

The discovery mechanism is host-runtime-specific -- read
`adapters/runtime/<platform>/install-skill.md` for how to locate prior
installs on the current platform. The adapter is responsible for whatever
lookup is idiomatic on its platform (scanning a conventional package home,
reading a per-runtime index, following pointer files for custom install
locations, etc.). This spec does not prescribe a single detection path.

**Result:**
- **Found:** the adapter returns the `config_dir` of the existing install.
  Read `installed.json` at that path for the installed version. Go directly
  to the [## Upgrading](#upgrading) section at the end of this document.
  Apply only the changelog steps for versions after the installed version.
- **Not found:** proceed with the full install steps below. The configure
  wizard (Step 4) collects a NEW `install_dir` from the user.

## 2. What This Package Installs
*Tier: 2 (required outcome)*

After this step, the user knows what workflows this package provides.

**"start code review"** — Reads the current MR/PR diff, applies the review checklist, posts the structured review comment to GitLab or GitHub, and updates the Last Review artifact.

## 3. Dependency Check
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the AI MUST have verified that all required tools and MCP
dependencies are present. If any required dependency is missing, the install
is halted and the user is told what to install.

**Required tools:**
- [ ] **python >=3.9** — Runs post_review.py to call the GitLab or GitHub API.

## 4. Run Configuration Wizard
*Tier: 2 (required outcome)*

After this step, all configuration values declared in CONFIGURE.md have been
collected from the user and confirmed. Read and follow CONFIGURE.md.

All `{{config.*}}` references in subsequent steps are substituted with
the values collected here.

## 5. Resolve install_dir to an absolute path
*Tier: 1 (mechanical)*

Before writing any file or storing the value, expand `{{config.install_dir}}`
to a platform-native absolute path:

- **Cowork (Windows):** if the value contains `~`, replace it with the user's
  Windows home (typically the path under `C:/Users/USERNAME/` -- use the
  platform's native separator, double backslashes inside JSON files).
  Example: `~/.claude/packages/ai-code-review-flow/` -> the absolute path under
  `C:/Users/USERNAME/.claude/packages/ai-code-review-flow/`.
- **Linux / macOS:** if the value contains `~`, replace it with `$HOME` or
  `os.path.expanduser('~')`.

From this point on, treat `{{config.install_dir}}` as the absolute expanded
path. Persist the expanded value (not the user-typed literal) when you write
config.json (Step 6) and installed.json (Step 11). The runtime AI reads these
files via tools that do not expand `~`, so a literal tilde breaks runtime
config lookup.

## 6. Install Scripts
*Tier: 1 (mechanical)*

Create `{{config.install_dir}}` if it does not exist. Copy script files from
`scripts/` in this bundle to `{{config.install_dir}}/scripts/`.

- **`post_review`** — POST a structured review comment to a GitLab MR or GitHub PR via REST API.

## 7. Make skills discoverable to the host runtime
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the host runtime MUST be able to load `code-review` from
`components/skills/code-review/` when its trigger phrases appear ("start
code review", "review this PR", "review this MR", "submit review", "do code
review", "make a code review"). The procedure depends on the runtime; consult
`adapters/runtime/<your-platform>/install-skill.md` for platform knowledge
and a worked example.

**Cowork:** the adapter at `adapters/runtime/cowork/install-skill.md`
describes how Cowork's Personal Skills UI registers skills. The combined
SKILL.md, scripts, and artifact templates get packaged into a `.skill` zip
and installed via the "Save skill" button. The adapter also handles the
artifact outcome in Step 8 (via `mcp__cowork__create_artifact`).

**Codex:** the adapter at `adapters/runtime/codex/install-skill.md`
describes how Codex discovers skills via `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. **This is
critical** — placing files in `~/.codex/skills/` is not enough; the host
runtime must be told about them via AGENTS.md registration. Without the
AGENTS.md stanza, "start code review" will not trigger the skill (this was
the v1.2.x install failure mode).

**Claude Code / Cursor / Other platforms:** if your platform has a runtime
adapter directory, follow it. Otherwise apply the generic-copy convention:
copy `components/skills/code-review/` into your platform's skills directory.

- **Claude Code:** copy to `~/.claude/skills/`
- **Other platforms:** load the SKILL.md content as a persistent instruction set.

## 8. Make artifacts available on the host runtime
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the Last Review record MUST be in a form the user can open
and the runtime can update. On HTML-capable runtimes this means an artifact
card; on text-only runtimes it means a markdown fallback file at a known
path. Consult the runtime adapter for the platform's artifact mechanism.

**Cowork:** the artifact gets created during Step 7 via Step C3 of
`adapters/runtime/cowork/install-skill.md` (`mcp__cowork__create_artifact`).
Skip this step if you ran the Cowork adapter.

**Codex:** the adapter seeds `{{config.install_dir}}/last-review.md` from
`components/artifacts/last-review/artifact.md` during install. Skip this
step if you ran the Codex adapter.

**Claude Code / Cursor / other HTML-capable platforms:**

**last-review** (`components/artifacts/last-review/`)

- **HTML-artifact platforms (Claude.ai):**
  Create a new artifact using `artifact.html`. Name it "last-review".

- **CLI / file-based platforms (no runtime adapter available):**
  Use the description in `artifact.md` to maintain an equivalent
  file-based structure (e.g. a markdown table at a known path).

## 9. Make protocols invocable on the host runtime
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, each protocol's trigger phrase MUST be recognized by the
host runtime. Registration mechanism depends on the runtime.

**Cowork / Codex:** the protocol(s) are folded into the SKILL.md that the
runtime adapter installs in Step 7. Their trigger phrases live in the
skill's `description:` frontmatter (Cowork) or AGENTS.md stanza (Codex)
and the platform's matcher picks them up automatically. No separate
registration needed.

**Claude Code / Cursor:** read each protocol file and register it manually:

**`start-review.md`**
- Trigger: "start code review"
- Alias: "review this PR"
- Alias: "review this MR"
- Alias: "submit review"
- Alias: "do code review"
- Alias: "make a code review"
- Register as a slash command, skill, or remembered instruction.

## 10. Confirm Installation
*Tier: 2 (required outcome)*

After this step, the user MUST know what was installed, what was degraded
(if anything), and how to invoke the workflow.

Summarize to the user:

- Scripts installed
- Skill(s) installed and registered with the host runtime
- Artifact(s) set up
- Protocol registered: "start code review"
- Any missing dependencies and their impact on the workflow

**How to use:**
- Say **"start code review"** to trigger the start-review workflow.

## 11. Write installed.json
*Tier: 1 (mechanical)*

Write `{{config.install_dir}}/installed.json` with:

```json
{
  "package": "ai-code-review-flow",
  "version": "<NEW>",
  "installed_at": "<current ISO-8601 timestamp>",
  "platform": "<detected platform — cowork / claude-code / codex / etc.>",
  "a3ip_spec": "1.9",
  "config_dir": "{{config.install_dir}}",
  "install_method": "<cowork-skill | generic-copy>"
}
```

Per spec v1.9 (inherited from v1.8): `install_method` is required;
`upgraded_from`, `source_bundle`, and `backup_dir` are optional but should
be included by adapters that use them (see Cowork Step C6 and Codex Step C6
for examples).

This is the canonical install record. The next install/upgrade of this
package looks for this file at `{{config.install_dir}}/installed.json` —
if present, it runs the Upgrading flow instead of a fresh install.


---

## Platform-Specific Notes

### Cowork (primary platform)
Full support for artifacts, skills, and scheduled tasks. Skill installs via
the Personal Skills UI; the platform's matcher auto-discovers trigger
phrases from `description:` frontmatter.

### Codex
Skills live at `~/.codex/skills/<name>/` but require an AGENTS.md stanza to
be discoverable. The runtime adapter handles this; see Step 7.

### Claude Code (CLI)
Full protocol support. Artifacts degrade to markdown files. Use cron or a
system scheduler for automated tasks.

### Other platforms
Core protocols work on any platform. Adapt as needed.

---

## Upgrading

When `installed.json` is found at `{{config.install_dir}}/installed.json`,
follow these steps instead of the full install.

### Step U1 — Read installed version
Read `{{config.install_dir}}/installed.json`. Note the `version` field.

### Step U2 — Apply CHANGELOG steps
Find all version entries in `CHANGELOG.md` newer than the installed version.
Apply them in order from oldest to newest. For each version entry:
1. Check `### Breaking changes` — if any, tell the user before proceeding.
2. Check `### Config changes` — if new required keys, run the config wizard
   for those keys only.
3. Follow `### Upgrade steps` exactly.

### Step U3 — Update installed.json
After all upgrade steps complete, overwrite `{{config.install_dir}}/installed.json`
with the new `version` and `installed_at` timestamp. Preserve the existing
`platform`, `a3ip_spec`, and `config_dir` fields.

### Step U4 — Confirm
Tell the user the versions upgraded from and to, and the changes applied.

Do **not** run the full install sequence during an upgrade.
Apply only the delta steps listed in CHANGELOG.md.

## Uninstalling

Triggered when the user says "uninstall ai-code-review-flow", "remove
ai-code-review-flow", or equivalent. Uninstall is idempotent -- running it
when the package is not installed is a no-op (Step UN1 exits cleanly), not
an error.

### Step UN1 -- Locate the existing install
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the AI MUST know whether this package is installed on the
host, and if so where its `config_dir` lives. The discovery mechanism is
host-runtime-specific -- read `adapters/runtime/<platform>/uninstall-skill.md`
for how to locate prior installs on the current platform. This is the
symmetric counterpart of INSTALL.md Step 1.

- **Not found:** tell the user there is nothing to uninstall and exit
  cleanly. Steps UN2 onward are skipped.
- **Found:** read `version`, `platform`, `config_dir`, and `install_method`
  from `installed.json` at the discovered path. They drive every subsequent
  step.

### Step UN2 -- Show the Uninstall Plan and resolve per-key config policy
*Tier: 1 (mechanical)*

Present an Uninstall Plan listing:

- The skill files / artifacts / protocol registrations that will be removed
  (paths derived per the platform's `uninstall-skill.md`).
- The per-key disposition of every configuration value (see below).
- The `installed.json` file that will be removed.

**Resolving the config-key policy.** Walk every entry in the `configuration:`
block of `manifest.yaml` and consult its `preserve_on_uninstall:` field. For
this package:

| Key | Policy | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `install_dir` | `false` | Silently purged (path is meaningless after uninstall). |
| `platform` | `ask` | Prompt the user. |
| `project_ref` | `ask` | Prompt the user. |
| `api_token` | `true` | Silently preserved (PAT is hard to re-issue). |
| `base_url` | `ask` | Prompt the user (only if present). |

For each `ask` key, ask the user a focused per-key question (key label +
description + brief reminder of what's stored). Capture each answer.

End the plan with **Confirm to proceed.** If the user declines, abort --
nothing is removed.

### Step UN3 -- Make the skill un-discoverable to the host runtime
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the host runtime MUST NOT load the `code-review` skill in
response to its trigger phrases. Read
`adapters/runtime/<platform>/uninstall-skill.md` for the platform-specific
procedure (Cowork: remove the skill from Personal Skills UI; Codex: remove
the AGENTS.md stanza).

### Step UN4 -- Remove the Last Review artifact from the host runtime
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the host runtime MUST NOT surface the `last-review`
artifact. Read `adapters/runtime/<platform>/uninstall-skill.md` for the
artifact-removal mechanism on the current platform.

If at least one configuration key was preserved in Step UN2 (the user keeps
their API token + project context), the Last Review artifact MAY be
preserved at the AI's discretion -- its content is persisted state, not
fresh runtime output. The default is to remove unconditionally; defer to
the adapter if it overrides.

### Step UN5 -- Make `start review` no longer invocable
*Tier: 2 (required outcome -- adapter procedure)*

After this step, the host runtime MUST NOT recognize the "start code
review" trigger phrase or its aliases. On runtimes where protocol discovery
is a side-effect of skill discovery (Cowork's `description:` frontmatter
loaded with the skill), this is a no-op once UN3 completes. Read the
uninstall-skill.md for the platform-specific story.

### Step UN6 -- Remove install_dir contents
*Tier: 1 (mechanical)*

Apply the per-key decisions resolved in Step UN2:

1. Read the current `{{config.install_dir}}/config.json`.
2. Construct a new version containing ONLY the keys whose disposition is
   *preserve* (typically `api_token` + whichever keys the user kept). Write
   it back, OR remove the file if no keys survive.
3. For each *purge* key with `storage: env-var` or `storage: keychain`,
   also remove the value from that storage. Read the uninstall-skill.md
   for the platform's credential-removal mechanism.
4. Delete every other file and subdirectory in `{{config.install_dir}}`
   EXCEPT `config.json` (if preserved) and `installed.json`.

### Step UN7 -- Remove installed.json
*Tier: 1 (mechanical)*

Remove `{{config.install_dir}}/installed.json`. After this step, INSTALL.md
Step 1 / Uninstall Step UN1 will return "no install" on subsequent runs,
making the uninstall idempotent. If the install directory is empty after
this, also remove the empty directory.

### Step UN8 -- Confirm to the user
*Tier: 1 (mechanical)*

Tell the user the uninstall completed. Name:
1. The version that was uninstalled.
2. Which configuration keys were preserved (and where -- e.g.
   `{{config.install_dir}}/config.json` for the trimmed file, plus any
   keychain entries that survived).
3. Which keys were purged.
4. Any leftover state the user may want to know about (e.g. on Codex,
   the AGENTS.md stanza was removed and the file's other content is
   unchanged).

=== END FILE ===

